(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-acid-base-part-1-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Acid-Base Part I Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"When water combines with carbon dioxide in the body's main buffer, which acid forms?","choices":["Ammonium chloride","Carbonic acid","Hydrochloric acid","Lactic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Water and carbon dioxide combine to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into hydrogen and bicarbonate ions. The reaction runs both ways, which is how the lungs (carbon dioxide) and kidneys (bicarbonate) act on one buffer. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the normal anion gap?","choices":["3 to 6","9 to 15","22 to 26","36 to 44"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The normal anion gap is 9 to 15, calculated as sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate (the measured cation minus the measured anions). Serum albumin accounts for most of this normal gap. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which physical finding is associated with the hypokalemia of metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["Papilledema","Hyporeflexia","Asterixis","Kussmaul respirations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic alkalosis has no characteristic findings, but the associated hypokalemia causes weakness and hyporeflexia. Orthostatic hypotension may also be present. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 24, 38, 39, 50, 51"},{"q":"What restores a normal pH in an acid-base disorder?","choices":["Waiting for full compensation","Breathing into a paper bag","Giving oxygen to every patient","Correcting the underlying disorder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Compensation never returns the pH to normal, so the underlying disorder must be corrected to restore a normal pH. For every simple disorder, treatment starts with the cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the first-line management of acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Breathe into a paper bag","Wait for the kidneys to compensate","Restore ventilation and treat the cause","Give a bicarbonate infusion first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory acidosis is treated by treating the underlying cause. In acute disease the priority is restoring ventilation, because hypoventilation is what raises the carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which effect is listed for a pH beyond the sustainable range?","choices":["Joint swelling","Skin rash","Seizures","Hair loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pH beyond the sustainable range (below 6.7 or above 7.6) may cause cardiac hypocontractility or irritability, seizures, cerebral edema and metabolic disturbances, so seizures are listed among the effects. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which thoracic trauma is listed as a cause of acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":["High altitude exposure","Heat exposure","Flail chest","Rib contusion with normal breathing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic trauma causes acute respiratory acidosis through pneumothorax or flail chest, which impair ventilation. Hypoventilation lets carbon dioxide build up, the pH falls, and the picture is respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the normal range for serum bicarbonate?","choices":["9 to 15 mEq/L","12 to 18 mEq/L","22 to 26 mEq/L","36 to 44 mEq/L"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Normal serum bicarbonate is 22 to 26 mEq/L, with 24 as the working midpoint. It sits beside the other normal blood gas values: arterial carbon dioxide 36 to 44 mm Hg and pH 7.35 to 7.45. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 47"},{"q":"Over what time scale does the renal mechanism adjust plasma bicarbonate?","choices":["Weeks to months","After about a year","Hours to days","Seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The renal mechanism controls plasma bicarbonate by excreting or retaining filtered bicarbonate in urine, and it works over hours to days, slower than the lungs' control of carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"In acidosis, what happens to oxygen delivery to the tissues?","choices":["It stops entirely","It is unaffected","It increases","It decreases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acidosis decreases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and shifts the dissociation curve to the right, so hemoglobin releases more oxygen to the tissues and oxygen delivery increases. Alkalosis does the reverse. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How far do compensatory mechanisms move the pH?","choices":["Toward normal but never back to normal","Fully back to normal","Past normal to the opposite side","Further from normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The body attempts to correct a disturbance, but compensation never returns the pH to normal and never corrects it beyond normal. The pH moves toward normal without reaching it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Beyond which arterial pH values is the state unsustainable?","choices":["Below 6.7 or above 7.6","Below 7.35 or above 7.45","Below 7.0 or above 7.5","Below 6.0 or above 8.0"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pH below 6.7 or above 7.6 is unsustainable. It may cause cardiac hypocontractility or irritability, seizures, cerebral edema and metabolic disturbances, and may warrant therapy to normalize pH along with treating the underlying cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A rise in carbon dioxide leads to which change in breathing?","choices":["No change in breathing","Increased ventilation","Decreased ventilation","Breath holding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A rise in carbon dioxide lowers cerebral pH, and the brain stem chemoreceptor answers by increasing ventilation, which blows the carbon dioxide off and corrects the change. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"An elevated anion gap is usually due to which change?","choices":["A decrease in unmeasured anions","An increase in unmeasured cations","An increase in serum chloride","An increase in unmeasured anions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An elevated anion gap is usually due to an increase in unmeasured anions. Less often it is due to a decrease in unmeasured cations (calcium, magnesium, potassium, gamma-globulins). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Where is the chemoreceptor that regulates respiration in response to carbon dioxide?","choices":["In the brain stem","In the kidney","In the liver","In the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A chemoreceptor in the brain stem regulates respiration. Increased carbon dioxide lowers cerebral pH, which the brain stem answers with increased ventilation to clear the carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis signal about the patient's fluid status?","choices":["Extracellular volume contraction","Euvolemia or hypervolemia","Isolated hyperkalemia","Normal fluid status"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis is a sign of extracellular volume contraction, which is why isotonic saline corrects the deficit. Saline-unresponsive alkalosis occurs with euvolemia or hypervolemia. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"In saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, what is the urine chloride?","choices":["Above 40 mEq/L","Exactly 100 mEq/L","The same as the serum chloride","Below 25 mEq/L"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Urine chloride is lower in saline-responsive alkalosis (below 25 mEq/L) and higher in saline-unresponsive alkalosis (above 40 mEq/L). The responsive type is the volume-contracted one. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which substance accounts for most of the physiologic (normal) anion gap?","choices":["Chloride","Bicarbonate","Sodium","Albumin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The anion gap is the concentration of anions other than chloride and bicarbonate. Serum albumin accounts for most of the normal gap; lactate, ketones, phosphate and sulfate also contribute. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Using about 2 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg acutely and 5 per 10 chronically, which bicarbonate fits acute respiratory alkalosis with a carbon dioxide of 30 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44, midpoint 40) from a baseline bicarbonate of 24?","choices":["19 mEq/L","22 mEq/L","24 mEq/L","26 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The carbon dioxide fell 10 mm Hg (40 to 30), and acute respiratory alkalosis lowers bicarbonate about 2 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg. So 24 - 2 = 22 mEq/L is the expected bicarbonate. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which cause of respiratory alkalosis works through hypoxia-driven hyperventilation?","choices":["Myasthenia gravis","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Flail chest","High altitude"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hyperventilation secondary to hypoxia causes respiratory alkalosis, and high altitude is a listed example. The others on that list are severe anemia, ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypotension. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In metabolic acidosis, the measured carbon dioxide is higher than the Winter formula predicts. What does that indicate?","choices":["A coexisting respiratory acidosis","A coexisting respiratory alkalosis","Appropriate compensation","Metabolic alkalosis instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If the actual carbon dioxide is higher than the Winter formula's expected value, the patient has metabolic acidosis together with respiratory acidosis. This can be a sign of impending respiratory failure. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"By the Winter formula, what is the expected carbon dioxide when bicarbonate is 10 mEq/L?","choices":["13 to 17 mm Hg","16 to 20 mm Hg","21 to 25 mm Hg","36 to 44 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x bicarbonate + 8. With bicarbonate 10: 1.5 x 10 + 8 = 15 + 8 = 23, plus or minus 2, so 21 to 25 mm Hg is the appropriately compensated value. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which way does acidosis shift the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve?","choices":["Upward without a shift","It does not shift","To the right","To the left"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acidosis shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right. Hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen falls, so more oxygen is delivered to the tissues. Alkalosis shifts the curve to the left. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106) and bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26). What is the calculated anion gap?","choices":["10","14","24","38"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = 138 - (114 + 14) = 138 - 128 = 10, within the normal 9 to 15. With a low bicarbonate and a high chloride, this is a normal-gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which describes a mixed acid-base disorder?","choices":["More than one primary disturbance at once","One primary disturbance plus compensation","A disorder with a normal pH","An acidosis that has fully compensated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A mixed acid-base disorder is more than one primary disturbance occurring at the same time, each with its own compensation. A simple disorder is one primary disturbance plus its corresponding compensation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which test separates saline-responsive from saline-unresponsive metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["Serum anion gap","Serum albumin","Arterial oxygen pressure","Urine chloride"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Urine chloride differentiates the two types. It is lower in saline-responsive alkalosis (below 25 mEq/L) and higher in saline-unresponsive alkalosis (above 40 mEq/L), as printed. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the usual course of hyperventilation causing respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Self-limited","Progressive without treatment","Fatal within hours","Chronic in most patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hyperventilation is usually self-limited. Treatment targets the underlying cause, and a paper bag is not necessary because it does not fix carbon dioxide and may lower oxygen. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What happens to serum potassium in alkalosis?","choices":["It is lost in the stool","It falls as potassium moves into cells","It rises as potassium leaves cells","It stays the same"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In alkalosis, hydrogen ions shift from the intracellular to the extracellular space while potassium shifts from the extracellular to the intracellular space. Serum potassium therefore falls, giving hypokalemia. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the first step in analyzing acid-base status?","choices":["Check the urine chloride","Look at the pH","Calculate the anion gap","Apply the Winter formula"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Analysis starts with the pH to decide acidosis or alkalosis. Then ask which of carbon dioxide or bicarbonate is consistent with it (respiratory or metabolic), whether it could be mixed, and what the compensation is. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which endocrine state is a listed cause of respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Hypothyroidism","Primary aldosteronism","Adrenal insufficiency","Pregnancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The endocrine causes of respiratory alkalosis are pregnancy and hyperthyroidism. Septicemia, heat exposure and hepatic failure are also listed among the causes, and all drive hyperventilation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
